s importante no solo para la salud celular, sino también para la salud ósea y la regulación de los niveles de magnesio. \n\nLa vitamina E actúa protegiendo las membranas lipídicas y ha sido estudiada por su potencial en la salud cerebral, incluyendo su relación con la cognición y la demencia. Además, se ha observado que puede ayudar a reducir el estrés oxidativo en pacientes con enfermedades inflamatorias intestinales durante episodios moderados. \n\nSin embargo, se debe tener precaución con su uso en dosis altas, especialmente en pacientes que reciben quimioterapia o radioterapia, ya que no se recomienda en estas circunstancias. Las recomendaciones diarias sugieren una administración de 10 U.I. de vitamina E. También se incluye en la nutrición parenteral y en las pautas de nutrición infantil, destacando su importancia en diversas etapas de la vida y en diferentes condiciones de salud. \n\nEn resumen, la vitamina E es un antioxidante esencial que protege las células del daño, contribuye a la salud ósea y cerebral, y debe ser administrada con cuidado en ciertas condiciones médicas.\",28\r\n2005,VITAMINA C,\"Vitamin C, also known as Ascorbic Acid, is a water-soluble vitamin that plays a crucial role in various bodily functions. It is recognized for its antioxidant properties, which help protect cells from oxidative stress, and is essential for the growth and repair of tissues, including skin, bones, and connective tissue. Vitamin C acts as a cofactor for numerous enzymes and is involved in the conversion of dopamine to noradrenaline, contributing to brain health.\n\nThis vitamin is particularly important for enhancing the absorption of non-heme iron by reducing ferric (Fe3+) to ferrous (Fe2+) state, making it vital for individuals with iron deficiency. It is often recommended for patients with specific dietary conditions, such as those with glucose-galactose malabsorption or hereditary fructose intolerance, as it is found in foods that may be restricted for these individuals.\n\nIn clinical settings, Vitamin C supplementation may be necessary for patients with certain health conditions, including those undergoing cancer treatment, cirrhotic patients, and individuals with inflammatory bowel disease (EII). However, it is important to monitor intake to avoid exceeding recommended daily allowances, as excessive amounts can lead to increased urinary oxalate excretion and potentially contribute to kidney stone formation.\n\nVitamin C is sensitive to heat and storage conditions, which can degrade its nutritional quality. It is commonly included in multivitamin formulations for parenteral nutrition and is available in various supplement forms, such as Ácido Ascórbico Bayer and Redoxc. Overall, Vitamin C is an essential nutrient that supports immune function, skin health, and overall well-being, and its intake should be carefully managed, especially in critical patients.\",34\r\n164,ENFERMEDAD DE ALZHEIMER,\"Alzheimer's disease, known as \"\"enfermedad de Alzheimer\"\" in Spanish, is the most common form of dementia, primarily affecting the elderly population. It is characterized by a complex pathophysiology and is significantly influenced by age, which is the main risk factor. This neurodegenerative condition leads to severe cognitive decline, including memory loss and behavioral changes. Additionally, it can result in significant swallowing difficulties, impacting up to 80% of patients. Research suggests that Alzheimer's disease may also be influenced by diet and other modifiable risk factors, highlighting the importance of lifestyle choices in its management and prevention.\",16\r\n3447,ENFERMEDAD CORONARIA,\"\"\"ENFERMEDAD CORONARIA,\"\" or coronary disease, is a medical condition characterized by the reduction of blood flow to the heart, primarily due to the buildup of plaque in the coronary arteries. This condition is often associated with obesity and metabolic health issues. Dietary factors play a significant role in the development and management of coronary disease, particularly the intake of dietary fats. The balance of omega-3 and omega-6 fatty acids, as well as the consumption of alpha-linolenic acid (ALA) and fish rich in omega-3 fatty acids, can influence the risk of developing this condition. Overall, coronary disease is linked to various cardiovascular risk factors and can be mitigated through dietary choices.\",7\r\n3440,CÁNCER DE COLON,\"CÁNCER DE COLON, or colon cancer, is a type of cancer that can be influenced by various dietary factors. Research indicates that a diet rich in fiber, vitamin E, and vitamin C may play a significant role in the prevention of this disease. Specifically, an intake of 60 mg of vitamin C per day has been associated with a reduced risk of developing colon cancer, as highlighted by epidemiological studies. Additionally, the effects of omega-3 fatty acids on the development of colon cancer have also been a subject of research. Overall, colon cancer is a health risk that is closely linked to dietary intake, and it is a concern that is addressed in nutritional guidelines and recommendations.\",5\r\n843,DRE,\"DRE, or disease-related malnutrition, is a condition that can significantly impact patients' health and is influenced by the quality of food provided in hospitals. In particular, DRE is identified and evaluated in patients with Alzheimer’s disease to tailor their nutritional treatment effectively. This highlights the importance of addressing nutritional needs in the management of various health conditions, ensuring that patients receive appropriate dietary support to improve their overall well-being.\",4\r\n12367,EII,\"EII refers to inflammatory bowel disease, encompassing conditions such as Crohn's disease and ulcerative colitis that affect the digestive tract.\",15\r\n15359,ADOLESCENTES,\"Adolescents experience increased nutritional demands, particularly for iron and calcium, due to growth spurts.\",2\r\n7296,FRUTAS Y VERDURAS,\"Frutas y verduras son